# Line-ending policy for content-hashed artifacts.
#
# PROTOCOL.md §3 requires generated tables and figures to carry content hashes.
# A hash only means something if the bytes are identical everywhere. Without
# these rules, a commit from Windows (core.autocrlf=true) stores LF, checks out
# CRLF locally and LF in Linux CI, and every recorded SHA-256 disagrees with the
# file it describes — which is exactly what broke CI on 2026-08-15.
#
# Scoped deliberately to generated artifacts and shell scripts rather than a
# repo-wide `* text=auto eol=lf`: the global form would renormalize every tracked
# text file at once, producing a large one-time diff that buries real changes.
# Consider adopting it separately, as its own commit.

# Generated artifacts whose bytes are hashed. Never translate these.
results/**/*.json  -text
results/**/*.tex   -text
results/**/*.csv   -text
results/**/*.md    -text

# Binaries: never translate, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF or they will not execute on Linux.
*.sh   text eol=lf
